Demote the missing SPI_CONTROLLER_ENABLE_CS_GPIOD #warning to a runtime warning

SPI_CONTROLLER_ENABLE_CS_GPIOD is not a mainline kernel flag; it is added
by a Morse Micro patch to include/linux/spi/spi.h that only the bcm27xx
target carries (991-0007-spi-support-control-cs-pin-on-init.patch).

Since CONFIG_MORSE_SPI=y is now hardcoded so the SPI bus front-end is
always built, spi.c also compiles on targets without that kernel patch
(e.g. ramips/mt76x8 for ht-hd01-v2), where the driver's #warning fallback
is fatal under the kernel's -Werror.

Replace the compile-time #warning with a runtime warning in the SPI probe
path. On kernels with the patch the flag is still set as before; on
kernels without it the message only ever appears if a radio is actually
probed over SPI, which is exactly the case where it is relevant.

--- a/spi.c
+++ b/spi.c
@@ -1510,7 +1510,12 @@
 #ifdef SPI_CONTROLLER_ENABLE_CS_GPIOD
 	spi->controller->flags |= SPI_CONTROLLER_ENABLE_CS_GPIOD;
 #else
-#warning "SPI_CONTROLLER_ENABLE_CS_GPIOD macro not defined"
+	/* Flag is added by an out-of-tree spi.h patch which not all target
+	 * kernels carry. Without it CS may be forced high during spi_setup,
+	 * which only matters when a radio is actually attached over SPI.
+	 */
+	MORSE_SPI_WARN(mors,
+		       "SPI_CONTROLLER_ENABLE_CS_GPIOD not supported by this kernel; CS init sequencing may be unreliable\n");
 #endif
 #endif
 	morse_spi_xfer_init(mspi);
